Quoting  Janis Papanagnou  <Janis_Papanagnou@hotmail.com>:
>David Damerell wrote:
>>Well, that's part of the problem here, no-one's defining "balance". I am
>>particularly curious about Janis's definition.
>I can't claim to have any normative definition. But it's the many design
>issues that reveal _advantages vs. drawbacks_ of decisions you make in the
>game,

Now that's certainly something that fits my definition, but not - in my
view - always fits the game. Back to alchemy again; the advantage is
colossal and the drawbacks are essentially non-existent. You need a
well-organised stuff analysis operation anyway and you don't give up
anything by turning those PoH and PoEH into PoFH.

The advantage of two-weapon combat is colossal and again the drawbacks are
minimal now that SDSM means more slots can grant reflection.

Another aspect of how I would define balance is the payoff between
advantage and skill. A technique which is trivial to describe and execute
should bring a trivial benefit; it should not bring a great benefit and
particularly it should not be indefinitely repeatable for an indefinite
benefit. 

The real interest and skill in roguelikes is in dealing with unfamiliar
situations, and the replay value of roguelikes stems from how their
random elements throw up those situations. The game would be balanced if
great benefits required you to show skill in overcoming threats.

Pudding farming - easily described, easily executed, no threat - is the
most glaring example of the imbalance here. Getting an endless supply of
items should require the player to fight a supply of threatening monsters.

>and all the _non-static situations_ that you cannot perfectly rely
>on (you know what I mean?),

I do, and I allude to them above, but I would hestitate to ascribe balance
to them. One problem roguelikes have is treading the fine line between
"insufficiently random" (Angband), "insufficiently challenging"
(NetHack middle game), and "occasionally just kills you" (Crawl).

I assert that a roguelike _must_ have at least one of those problems, not
that a poor design might not have more than one or a needless amount of
one. But I would say that "insufficiently challenging" is a kind of
imbalance; the player's capabilities are not well balanced with the
threats he faces - and NetHack's middle game quite blatantly has such an
imbalance. "occasionally just kills you" is obviously the other face of
that imbalance.

But that suggests that a more balanced game will be insufficiently random,
and as such that balance actually fights against those interesting random
situations.

However, I think NetHack's early game is generally pretty good from a
balance point of view; the situations are random, there are very few
unavoidable deaths, but it's not a cakewalk.

>and last but not least the _probabilities of monster and object generation_,

Object generation; sometimes you get a wand of wishing early on and you
get a pretty free run through the hardest part of the game. Sometimes you
find scrolls of genocide, sometimes you have to fight a hojillion
arch-liches. If you are Lawful sometimes you find a longsword early on and
get a big boost in combat power. Whoever you are, sometimes you get an
early altar and an easy route to a powerful weapon; sometimes you get no
unguarded altar until you are strong enough to off the Minetown Priest.
Sometimes you get the identify spell early and that breaks the back of the
stuff analysis game. Is that balanced?

Monster generation; sometimes the game generates the master mind flayer of
the Mines or a monster polytrapped into an arch-lich and it rips your head
off and spits down your neck. Sometimes it doesn't. The game _can't_ make
sensible decisions about what monster to generate because the internal
representation of monster difficulty doesn't match actual threat.
Sometimes you have to fight Demogorgon; sometimes you don't. Ditto
Pestilence.

Here's another part of my definition of balance again; the difficulty does
not vary wildly from game to game, except in the respect of intentional
variation like class/race differences. NetHack fails badly there; and,
although the difficulty of a roguelike is bound to be a bit lumpy, it
fails much more badly than it has to.

>and the _tuned interaction of objects_.

The tuned interaction of objects is very jolly, but does it promote
balance? I do love the depth of detail in NetHack, but I would have to
admit it is one of the usual suspects for game-unbalancing interactions;
when some random Dev Team member made puddings split, did they dream that
people would split them 48 million times?

>The game is in so many respects thoughtfully tuned that even if there are
>(especially for the advanced players in the late game) a few issues that
>seem (or are) unbalanced the game is overall very well balanced; or call
>it tuned - the game features are tuned against each other (as far as that
>is possible in a complex game) so that most interactions don't provide
>extreem situation (like too easy or too difficult play).

Well, I definitely disagree that that is the case. Some early game
occurrences provide a very easy experience (early WoW); in the late game
it does not take a great deal of playing skill (as opposed to a bit of
spoiler-reading) to reduce every monster you face to either Demogorgon or
a blot on the windshield; and there's a vast gap in the middle game,
between getting decent AC and a decent weapon and Gehennom (and maybe
later) where the game is just a quiet stroll in the park with the birds
singing etc.

>Do you have some good definition, maybe?

I hope I've discussed that above.
